1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,274
A useful Bittensor subnet starts with a simple question: what work should this network reward?

2
00:00:06,374 --> 00:00:11,712
The difficult part is turning that answer into a system people can inspect.

3
00:00:11,812 --> 00:00:17,150
Today, a builder moves between documentation, editors, terminals, evaluation scripts and chain explorers.

4
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:28,444
TensorStudio connects that journey in one browser workspace, so a team can spend more attention on the quality of its idea and less on connecting its tools.

5
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:31,756
Start with a conversation.

6
00:00:31,856 --> 00:00:36,145
Describe the problem, explore tradeoffs, and get practical next steps.

7
00:00:36,245 --> 00:00:43,168
Carry the idea into the Subnet builder, where editable files sit beside a working application preview.

8
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:48,874
Here, we add tasks to a real generated interface and inspect its code.

9
00:00:48,974 --> 00:00:57,214
That same loop helps a subnet team develop the tools around its service: describe, build, try, understand, and improve.

10
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:02,515
Then make the subnet's task explicit.

11
00:01:02,615 --> 00:01:05,868
Supply labelled examples for question answering or classification.

12
00:01:05,968 --> 00:01:14,250
Subnet setup produces a structured recipe for the task, ground truth, scoring and proposed weights, then assembles reviewed project templates.

13
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:18,860
The offline harness compares controlled answers and exposes the scoring behavior.

14
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:22,632
Its timing is simulated, but its calculations are real.

15
00:01:22,732 --> 00:01:28,500
This helps teams challenge an evaluation design before trusting the incentives built around it.

16
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:32,552
The architecture keeps each responsibility clear.

17
00:01:32,652 --> 00:01:36,806
The browser contains the workspace, preview and encrypted wallet tools.

18
00:01:36,906 --> 00:01:41,060
The web application handles validated assistant requests and owner access.

19
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:47,015
An authenticated Python runner assembles projects, evaluates fixtures, runs native processes and streams logs.

20
00:01:47,115 --> 00:01:49,567
A configurable model endpoint supplies inference.

21
00:01:49,667 --> 00:01:52,545
Separately, direct Subtensor reads supply network observations.

22
00:01:52,645 --> 00:01:58,500
Short lived runner sessions connect these components without placing server credentials in the browser.

23
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:10,085
Bittensor gives this workflow its purpose: specialized tasks, miners that perform the work, validators that assess contributions, and a chain that coordinates participation.

24
00:02:10,185 --> 00:02:13,592
TensorStudio maps those roles into inspectable development tools.

25
00:02:13,692 --> 00:02:20,169
Generated processes use signed HTTP requests, while TensorScan shows live blocks, events and subnet data.

26
00:02:20,269 --> 00:02:28,500
A running process is distinct from a registered subnet; chain participation, accepted weights and rewards need their own validation.

27
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:35,783
Our opportunity is developer productivity: team workspaces, managed runners, private evaluations and operational visibility.

28
00:02:35,883 --> 00:02:41,565
These are proposed paid products, with pricing grounded in actual compute and support costs.

29
00:02:41,665 --> 00:02:47,348
The current demonstration uses an ephemeral free runner; production teams need durable, isolated infrastructure.

30
00:02:47,448 --> 00:02:52,717
TensorStudio's vision is simple: make useful intelligence easier to build, evaluate and understand.

31
00:02:52,817 --> 00:02:58,500
Explore the workspace, inspect the demo, and bring your next Bittensor idea to life.
